You need to SSH into your EC2 instance, run pg_dump, then download the file. Here are the steps:

Option 1: SSH in and dump, then download

powershell

# 1. SSH into your EC2
ssh -i your-key.pem ec2-user@your-ec2-ip

# 2. On the EC2, create the dump
pg_dump -U postgres -d postgres -F p -f /tmp/backup.sql

# 3. Exit SSH
exit

# 4. Download the file to your local machine
scp -i your-key.pem ec2-user@your-ec2-ip:/tmp/backup.sql D:\workspace\ksp\backup.sql
Option 2: One-liner from local (dump directly via SSH tunnel)

powershell

# Create SSH tunnel to RDS/PostgreSQL on EC2
ssh -i your-key.pem -L 5433:localhost:5432 ec2-user@your-ec2-ip

# In another terminal, dump via the tunnel
pg_dump -h localhost -p 5433 -U postgres -d postgres -F p -f D:\workspace\ksp\backup.sql
Option 3: If PostgreSQL is on the EC2 itself (not RDS)

powershell

# Single command: SSH + pg_dump + pipe to local file
ssh -i your-key.pem ec2-user@your-ec2-ip "pg_dump -U postgres -d postgres" > D:\workspace\ksp\backup.sql
Replace:

your-key.pem → your actual SSH key file path
your-ec2-ip → your EC2 public IP or DNS
postgres → your actual DB name (from your prod .env it uses $DB_URL)
If you're using AWS RDS instead of PostgreSQL on EC2, you'd connect to the RDS endpoint from the EC2 instance:

bash

pg_dump -h your-rds-endpoint.rds.amazonaws.com -U postgres -d your_db_name -F p -f /tmp/backup.sql
